-### 1113 — Local-runtime architecture revision 8: the `Vendor` contract itself couldn't carry what six revisions assumed
-- ROOT CAUSE (independent revision-7 review): `Vendor.Models()` takes zero arguments and is contractually required not to vary between calls (`Registry.Register` calls it exactly once, permanently admits the snapshot) — every revision through 7 designed a `loadConfig(projectRoot)` loader with mtime-driven cache invalidation that this same interface makes impossible to implement. `AvailabilityQuery` carries only `Model`/`Home`, no `RuntimeID` — so a vendor serving more than one declared runtime (which `local-qwen` becomes the moment a second local profile exists) cannot tell which one an `Availability` call is about.
-- FIX: deleted the project-local tier from `local-models.toml` entirely — it's global-only now, because the pointer it stores (which local `agents-infra` project/profile owns the weights on this machine) is a machine-level fact, not a per-calling-Story-worktree one. That single change removes "project root" as a fact any `Vendor` method needs, which is what makes `Models()`'s call-stability honestly satisfiable (lazy, memoized forever, exactly like every other vendor's static catalog). `AvailabilityQuery` gains one field, `Runtime RuntimeID` — the identical fix this codebase already shipped once for `pkg/providerlimits.VerdictQuery`, built for the exact same problem (`google` already serves both `gemini` and `agy` under one `VendorID`).
-- FINDING: task-board's real closed-gate surface is nine items, not the four the review named — critically `modelDisplayOrder` PANICS THE ENTIRE BINARY at `init()` if any vendor-declared model isn't in a hand-written display-order list (not a per-launch refusal — a boot-time crash), and `pkg/remoteconfig/reasoningeffort` deliberately carries zero imports beyond stdlib to avoid a cycle with the model registry, so it can't just import `vendorplugin` to check a model's reasoning-effort requirement generically.
-- FINDING: task-board's tracked spawn run executes inside a detached background OS process reached only via a manifest-file handoff — no `context.Context` from the CLI invocation can ever cross that boundary, structurally, not by oversight. The codebase's own two existing "shell out with a bounded timeout" call sites (`launch_composition.go`, `agy_preflight.go`) already accept this and always locally re-wrap whatever `ctx` they're handed in `context.WithTimeout` — revision 8 adopts that exact precedent for `BuildLaunch`'s new `Preflight` step rather than pretending real upstream cancellation exists.
-- DECISION: split Preflight's admit/refuse table from Availability's UX-facing mapping. A positively-absent broker (nobody has cold-started Process B yet — the ordinary first-launch case) is `Unreachable` for UX purposes but explicitly ADMITS for Preflight — reusing one table for both questions was revision 7's actual bug, not a wording problem.
-- STATUS: revision 8 published (empty-tree CR, unchanged-tree validation: vet/build/test/regress all exit 0), handed to independent review. `TASK-260829-188jcf` checklist items 1-2 (stale `LaunchFactContributor` wording, found still live on the board by the review) superseded via new items 3-5 rather than deleted — this task-board version has no checklist-item-edit mutation.
