-### 0730 — Local-runtime identity design: split the turn from the server, and don't trust the reference prototype's identity model
-- DECISION: `TASK-260829-3jlxed` (STORY-260829-37w91q, add-pi-local-model-runtime-plane). Two processes, kept structurally apart: Process A (one Pi CLI turn) fits the existing Layer-1 `agentic.System` contract unchanged, no new lifecycle. Process B (the long-running local model server) needs a brand-new `pkg/localsupervisor` — the first process-executing code this module will ever carry, deliberately mirrored on the relux-agents-infra reference's own `model-harness`/Pi-launch-code separation rather than invented from scratch.
-- DECISION: `Publisher`/`Family` land as additive, legal-blank string fields on the shared `vendorplugin.Model` struct (same non-breaking evolution path v0.2.0 used for `Lifecycle`/`Pricing`/etc). `Backend`/executable/argv stay private to a new local-models-only `RuntimeSpec` type — never added to the shared `Model` contract three of four vendors would never use.
-- FINDING: the real "Pi" prototype in `relux-agents-infra` embodies exactly the anti-pattern this design exists to avoid. Its `Vendor` field is hardcoded to one of `openai|anthropic|qwen` (`project_config.go:381,406`) — so a locally-hosted Qwen checkpoint's vendor is literally the string `"qwen"`, identical to the cloud API's vendor id. No separate publisher or backend axis exists anywhere in that codebase; "mlx" only ever appears as free text inside a hand-typed `argv` array. The brief's constraint ("do not reuse Alibaba cloud auth/quota for a local resource") isn't precautionary — it's a real, present bug in the thing being used as a reference.
-- FINDING: the reference prototype's readiness-polling (exact-match model id + concurrent exit-watch), restart-with-budget supervision (rolling window + fatal-substring kill), and stale-PID reclaim (compare UID+PGID+start-time+exec-shape, never trust a bare PID) are all proven, working, safe to reuse. Quarantine and log rotation are 100% absent from it — greenfield, not "already solved elsewhere."
-- ROOT CAUSE (caught by independent review, fixed before handoff): first draft claimed "`local-models` vendor only reads supervisor state... true by construction, not by convention" — false. `Vendor.Availability()`/`Spawn()` are plain Go methods; nothing stops an implementation from calling `Ensure`/`Stop` from inside `Spawn()`. Fixed by splitting a narrow `StatusReader` interface (construction-time, read-only) from a separate `Supervisor` handle that only CLI/task-board wiring may ever hold, plus a dedicated import-graph regression test (test plan case 14). Lesson: "X is true by construction" is a claim about a type, not a sentence — say so only when a type actually enforces it.
-- STATUS: design handed off to review (to-review). Four open questions flagged for explicit sign-off rather than force-decided: per-family vs. shared `RuntimeID` for local models; `Availability` state mapping for busy/quarantined (no 5th state exists); quarantine/log-rotation numeric defaults are unvalidated starting points; whether `pkg/localsupervisor` belongs in this module at all vs. entirely in the consumer (the single biggest architectural call in the doc).
-- SCOPE: `.task-board` outcome resources on `TASK-260829-3jlxed` (`_architecture-decision.md`, `_parity-inventory.md`, `_adversarial-test-plan.md`). No production code touched. No live local-model process interacted with.
