-### 2015 — A guard's scan scope is part of its rule, not plumbing
-- ROOT CAUSE: `moduleSources` excluded directories by NAME — `.git`, `.temp`, `.task-board`, `.claude`, `.codex`, `vendor`, `node_modules`, `testdata` — which is a list of the machine-local trees somebody had thought of by then. A bootstrapped checkout has agents-infra installed at `.agents/`: gitignored, present only where agents-infra installed it, carrying its own `go.mod`, and dispatching on `"codex"`/`"claude"`. The guard reported 12 violations there and `go test ./...` went red on main while every worktree stayed green. The suite's colour depended on whose machine ran it.
-- NOTE: Timestamp is real local MSK; earlier entries today were stamped from a clock ~1.5h ahead, so this sits above them by append order, not by stamp.
-- FIX: `skipModuleDir` mirrors `go/build`'s own rules instead of naming directories — leading `.` or `_`, plus any subtree carrying its own `go.mod`. `vendor`/`node_modules`/`testdata` stay named. The root is never skipped on its own name: this checkout habitually lives under `.temp/`, and testing the root would scan zero files while reporting clean. `pkg/agentic/singlesource_guard_test.go`, and the duplicate walk at `pkg/vendorplugin/double_test.go:349`.
-- DECISION: `testdata` is EXCLUDED, matching `go build`. The scan set and the build set become the same set with no third rule; the cost is that a binding parked in `testdata` goes unreported. A guard fixture wanting a violating source writes it into a temp tree whose root it also controls instead. Argued in the `# Scan scope` threat-model section, pinned by a `testdata` case nested inside a scanned package.
-- FINDING: The exclusion is directional and BOTH directions are failures — too wide reddens trunk on machine-local code, too narrow silently unguards a package while CI stays green. So every case is planted twice: the SAME violating source inside a dot-dir, an underscore-dir, a nested module, `testdata` and `vendor` (must not be reported) and in two ordinary packages (must be). `pkg/agentic/singlesource_scanscope_test.go`.
-- FINDING: The load-bearing test is the one that runs FIRST — `TestSingleSourceGuardScanScopeFixtureIsViolating` scans every planted path directly, bypassing the walk, and requires all ten reported. Without it, every "not reported" assertion is equally consistent with a fixture that violates nothing, and the exclusion could widen to swallow the whole module while the file stayed green. A skip assertion is worth exactly what the proof that the skipped thing WAS a violation is worth.
-- FINDING: A `stat` failing for a reason other than not-exist is propagated, not read as absence. Collapsing the two turns an unreadable subtree into a silently unscanned one that looks like a clean scan. `TestSkipModuleDirUnreadableIsNotAbsent` uses a 0000 directory and skips itself if the deny did not take effect, so it cannot pass vacuously.
-- NOTE: Bound proven by NARROWING and by WIDENING, not only by deleting. 5 mutants on `skipModuleDir`: dot rule deleted, nested-go.mod rule narrowed to never fire, exclusion widened to every subdirectory, root exemption removed, stat failure read as absence. All killed, each by the test claiming that class.
-- SCOPE: `pkg/agentic/singlesource_guard_test.go`, `pkg/agentic/singlesource_scanscope_test.go` (new), `pkg/vendorplugin/double_test.go`, `pkg/vendorplugin/scanscope_test.go` (new), `README.md`. No production source changed. 58-mutant matrix green (66 named subtests = 58 caught + 8 declared-open residuals).
-- STATUS: Resolved (BUG-260821-3o0xr3), handed to review. Verified against the real bootstrapped `.agents` tree: old walk exit 1 with `child_launch_composition.go:95` verbatim, new walk exit 0.
